{"the_clinical_adequacy_of_contours": "The clinical adequacy of contours refers to how well the segmented boundaries (contours) of medical images align with clinical requirements and standards. This includes factors like precision, accuracy, and the ability to capture critical anatomical details necessary for effective diagnosis and treatment planning."},
{"iou_and_dice_similarity_coefficient_dsc__do_not_differentiate_between__systematic_and_random_errors": "The predicate \"do not differentiate between\" indicates that IoU and DSC metrics fail to distinguish or make a clear separation between two specific types of errors in segmentation results."},
{"systematic_and_random_errors": "Systematic and random errors refer to different types of inaccuracies in segmentation results. Systematic errors are consistent and repeatable inaccuracies, while random errors are unpredictable and vary from instance to instance. These errors can significantly impact the reliability and clinical adequacy of segmentation models."},
{"iou_and_dice_similarity_coefficient_dsc__do_not_account_for__proximity_to_critical_structures": "The phrase \"do not account for\" indicates that IoU and Dice similarity coefficient (DSC) have limitations in their evaluation criteria. Specifically, these metrics fail to consider certain important factors that could impact the clinical relevance and accuracy of the segmentation results."},
{"proximity_to_critical_structures": "Proximity to critical structures refers to the closeness of segmented regions to important anatomical or pathological features within an image. In medical image segmentation, ensuring that segmentations are accurate near critical structures is vital for clinical decision-making and treatment planning."},
