{"size_shape_and_resolution": "The objects \"size, shape, and resolution\" are key attributes of imaging characteristics. Size refers to the dimensions of the imaged objects, shape describes the geometric form of these objects, and resolution indicates the level of detail that can be discerned in the image. These attributes vary based on the imaging modality and the pathology being imaged."},
{"imaging_characteristics__vary_based_on__imaging_modality_and_underlying_pathology": "The phrase \"vary based on\" indicates a dependency relationship where the imaging characteristics are influenced by certain factors. In this context, it suggests that the observed imaging characteristics are not constant but change according to specific conditions or variables."},
{"imaging_modality_and_underlying_pathology": "Imaging modality refers to the type of imaging technology used, such as MRI, CT scan, or X-ray, each of which has distinct properties and capabilities. Underlying pathology refers to the specific medical condition or disease being imaged, which can affect how the condition appears in the images. Together, these factors influence the imaging characteristics observed."},
